## Where the idea started

I didn't start this project with an RL algorithm in mind. I started it with an elevator.

While riding one, I had a random thought: what if an elevator could actually think about where it was going?

Instead of simply reacting to whoever presses a button first,

what if it could consider things like distance, where other passengers are going, how busy the building is, and which requests should be handled together?

I didn't have a solution to that question. I didn't even have a clear idea of how RL would fit into it.

But the idea stuck with me enough that I wanted to see it exist.

That is exactly the kind of idea I wanted the RL Playground to be able to handle:

something that starts as a random thought,
turns into a system that can be experimented with,
and only later reveals whether there is actually an interesting learning problem inside it.

So the first question wasn't:

> How do I train an RL agent to control elevators?

It was:

> What would I need to build if I wanted to experiment with elevator decision making?

That changed the direction of the project completely.

---

## Building the world before the agent

For the first phase, I deliberately decided not to build RL.

Before an agent can make decisions, there needs to be something for it to make decisions about.

I needed elevators that could move, passengers that could appear, requests that could wait, doors that could open and close, and a building whose state changed over time.

So I treated the elevator system as a simulation first.

The goal was to create something solid enough that I could later plug different decision making strategies into it and compare their behavior.

This also meant separating the simulation from the eventual learning problem.

The elevator should not know anything about RL.

The passenger generator should not know anything about the dispatcher.

The renderer should only show what is happening.

That gave me a few principles to work around.

### Simulation first

The simulation had to be able to run without the visual interface.

The renderer is there so I can see what the system is doing, but the simulation itself should contain the actual world state and rules.

This means I can eventually run thousands of experiments without needing to render every one of them.

### Time based physics

I didn't want elevators to simply jump from one floor to another every simulation tick.

An elevator has velocity. It accelerates, potentially reaches a cruising speed, and needs to decelerate before reaching its destination.

So movement is based on continuous position and velocity rather than treating a floor as a single unit of movement.

As an example, for braking the elevator uses:

$$
d_{brake} = \frac{v^2}{2a}
$$

This gives the simulation enough physical structure for different trips to behave differently.

A short trip might never reach maximum speed, while a longer trip can accelerate, cruise, and then brake.

The point isn't to create a perfect physical model of a real elevator.

It's to create a model that behaves consistently enough for experiments to mean something.

### Replaceable decision making

The dispatcher is treated separately from the physical simulation.

That makes it possible to swap strategies later without rebuilding the entire simulation.

The first dispatcher is intentionally simple:

assign a request to the nearest available elevator, with waiting requests forming a queue when all elevators are busy.

It's not supposed to be clever. It's a baseline.

### Don't build RL yet

This was probably the most important design decision.

I didn't want to force an RL formulation onto the project just because I've swamped myself learning RL.

At this point, I don't know what the best state representation should be, what the action space should look like, or even whether RL will be the most interesting way to approach the problem.

So I left those questions open.

The simulation comes first. If an interesting decision problem emerges from it, then I can build the learning problem around the environment rather than inventing an environment around the algorithm.

