## The specification

Once I knew the direction, I started defining what the simulation actually needed.

The initial version would have:

* 10 floors
* 3 elevators
* 6 passengers per elevator
* 3 meter floor height
* continuous elevator position
* acceleration and deceleration
* maximum elevator speed
* braking distance based stopping
* door opening, holding, and closing
* passenger origins and destinations
* waiting, boarding, riding, and arrival states
* randomly generated passenger traffic
* reproducible traffic through random seeds
* a simple baseline dispatcher
* waiting queues
* simulation statistics
* an event system for important state changes
* a Pygame renderer
* independent simulation and rendering clocks

I also defined responsibilities before writing the implementation.

For example, the elevator is responsible for physical state and movement.

The dispatcher is responsible for deciding which elevator handles a request.

The traffic generator creates passengers.

The simulation coordinates everything.

The renderer displays the current state.

None of these should quietly take over another module's job.

That might sound like ordinary software structure, but for this project it was part of the experiment.

If I wanted to change the decision making later, I needed to know exactly where that decision making lived.

---

## Designing for experiments

Another thing I wanted from the beginning was the ability to observe more than just whether the simulation works.

An elevator system can look fine while performing terribly.

So the simulation tracks things such as:

* passenger waiting time
* total journey time
* travel time
* maximum waiting time
* average waiting time
* completed passengers
* passengers currently waiting
* passengers currently inside elevators
* accumulated waiting time
* elevator state and movement

These metrics give me something to measure when I eventually start comparing strategies.

For example, a dispatcher might reduce average waiting time while making a small number of passengers wait much longer.

Another might move more passengers overall but create congestion at certain floors.

Those are experiments I can't really run if all I have is a moving elevator on a screen.

---

## What I expected to be difficult

At this stage, I didn't know what would actually break.

I expected the physical model to require some thought, especially around acceleration, braking, and stopping precisely at a floor.

I also expected the architecture to change once I started trying to implement more complicated dispatching behavior.

But I deliberately didn't try to solve every future problem in advance.

There was no point designing a perfect RL environment before I knew what the simulation would actually look like.

The specification was therefore detailed enough to give me a direction, but open enough to let the experiment change it.

---

## Turning the specification into code

Only after the design was clear did I move into implementation.

This is also where my development workflow for this project became different from some of my earlier projects.

I used AI heavily to turn the specification into an initial implementation.

That let me move from a written idea to a functioning simulation much faster than if I had manually written every part myself.

But the important part for me was what happened after the code existed.

I had to read it, understand the relationships between the modules, run it, inspect its behavior, find things that didn't make sense, and eventually restructure parts of it.

The AI gave me something concrete to work with.

Instead of spending most of the early stage typing out implementation details, I could spend more of my time asking:

* Does this architecture actually represent the system I designed?
* Does the physics behave the way I intended?
* Where should this responsibility live?
* What assumptions are hidden in the implementation?
* What happens when I change this part?
* What can I actually measure?

That became an important part of the experiment itself.

The goal wasn't to produce code as quickly as possible.

It was to get from "I wonder if an elevator could work like this" to a system that I could actually experiment on.

---

## What comes next

This describes what I wanted the system to be before I started digging deeply into the implementation.

The next step was therefore not immediately adding more features.

It was understanding what I had actually built.

Getting a system to run is one thing. Being able to trace its state, understand its architecture, and confidently change it is another.

